  "strategy": "We propose to search for an optimized sequence using a Sturm-Liouville approach and Chebyshev polynomial bases, which allows for analytical computation of gradients and eigenvalue-based initialization. The method will construct sequences based on eigenvalues of a discretized Laplace operator with variable boundary conditions and rotational symmetry, aiding both exploration and exploitation.\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\n   - Define sequences using Fourier-Chebyshev projection basis to enable smooth optimization.\n   - Use the evaluator's provided normalization (sum >= 0.01) but fix the scale to avoid multiplicative dependencies.\n   - Minimize the score directly by analytic projections and gradient-based methods.\n   - Establish boundary conditions that maximize spacing between peaks while minimizing overall convolution peak.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Sequences will be parameterized using first 15 Chebyshev polynomials:\n     T\u2080(x) = 1\n     T\u2081(x) = x\n     T\u2082(x) = 2x\u00b2 - 1\n     ... \n     (for series in [-1,1])\n   - Normalize coefficients to satisfy ||a||\u2081 = 1.\n   - Formulate contiguous eigenvalue-based segments rather than individual terms.\n   - Through polynomial transformation, we derive a total score function.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Start by initializing coefficients for length \u2248128.\n   - Convert coefficients to appropriate eigenvalue vectors.\n   - Apply polynomial smoothing to the coefficients.\n   - Use a projected gradient that operates multiplicatively on the Fourier transforms.\n   - Compute gradients via analytic transforms of Chebyshev coefficients.\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\n   - Use temperature parameter for gradient smoothing.\n   - Schedule temperature decay properly.\n   - Maintain 10-15 expansion factors between local and global consultations.\n   - Ensure expense distribution is 80% analytic, 20% experimental.\n\n5. Budget Estimate and Time Scheduling:\n   - Budget 1800 seconds for 16-adjacent Fourier-Chebyshev space.\n   - Distinguish alpha (early) and beta (final) phase contributions.\n   - Stagnation: if scores plateau after 100 iterations, restart with related boundary condition.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Evaluate final score using:\n     score = (2 * n * ||f||\u221e) / (||a||\u2081)^2\n   - Must return optimized list of nonnegative numbers.\n   - Maintain at least 10 identified improvement candidates during optimization.\n   - Drop coefficients where contributions are less than 0.1% of eigenvalues.",
